{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 42, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond inherited the county at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother served as regent until he came of age. During his early years as count he faced many challenges including internal dissent and external threats from Muslim powers. The County of Tripoli was one of the four major Crusader states and Raymond III played a key role in its survival and diplomacy. He was known for his cautious nature and preference for negotiation over war.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a prominent figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem after he was named regent in 1174 during the minority of King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. As regent Raymond was responsible for the defense and administration of the kingdom. He was involved in several military campaigns against Saladin the Sultan of Egypt and Syria. Raymond often clashed with other nobles including the powerful Hospitallers and the Templars. His leadership was criticized by some for being too hesitant. He attempted to form alliances with Muslim leaders to prevent war but these efforts were often undermined by internal rivalries and mistrust among the Crusader nobility.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III was eventually removed from the regency in 1176 due to political pressure and replaced by the King’s uncle Raymond of Antioch. He returned to Tripoli and focused on rebuilding its defenses and economy. In 1182 he was briefly restored as regent but was removed again due to his reluctance to engage Saladin in open battle. Raymond was present at the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army was decisively defeated. He escaped the battlefield but died shortly after in Tripoli. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. Raymond III is remembered as a cautious and politically astute leader who struggled to balance the demands of war diplomacy and internal politics in a turbulent time. His legacy is one of quiet resilience amid the collapse of the Crusader states.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.0